# OJHunt Lite — AI Agent Guide

OJHunt Lite queries competitive programming Online Judge platforms and returns
solved-problem statistics. Use the API to look up any user across 28+ OJs and
compute deduplicated totals.

## Workflow

To get a user's deduplicated solved-problem totals across platforms:

1. Call GET /api/crawlers/ to list available crawlers.
2. For each relevant crawler, call GET /api/crawlers/{crawler}/{username}.
   These calls are independent and can run in parallel.
   A user may have different usernames on different platforms — each call
   carries its own crawler/username pair.
3. Collect all responses (including errors — /api/merge skips them).
4. POST the full array of responses to /api/merge.
   Do not call /api/merge until all crawler queries have completed.
   The merge step handles aggregator cross-platform deduplication server-side.

## CLI alternative (local installation)

If you can install Python packages, the CLI is simpler for pure stats queries:

  pip install ojhunt          # or: pipx install ojhunt / uv tool install ojhunt
  ojhunt tourist@codeforces tourist@atcoder --json

- Runs all queries in parallel; outputs JSON to stdout, progress to stderr
- summary.unique_solved is already deduplicated (same logic as /api/merge)
- No running server required

Run `ojhunt --help` for full options, JSON output schema, and jq examples.

CLI vs HTTP API:
- Simple stats query -> CLI preferred (one command, no server dependency)
- PDF generation or history tracking -> HTTP API only (server-side endpoints)
- Server-managed credentials (e.g. VJudge on the hosted site) -> HTTP API
- Login-required crawlers with your own credentials -> CLI (user:pass@crawler syntax)

## Tracking progress over time

To generate a PDF progress report with accumulated history, use the web UI at
{{ base }}/ rather than the API directly. The UI handles PDF caching, history
merging, and download automatically:

1. Add crawlers and usernames, then click "Query All".
2. Once all queries complete, click "Download Report".
   If a previous report was uploaded, its history is merged into the new PDF.
3. Save the downloaded PDF — upload it on the next run to carry history forward.

If you need to restore settings from a previous PDF (e.g. to re-run the same
crawlers), upload it via the "Upload previous report" button before querying.

## PDF tools (form endpoints)

These endpoints accept standard HTML form submissions and return application/pdf directly.
They are usable by agents via curl or HTTP clients.

POST {{ base }}/pdf/legacy
  Download a historical PDF for a user from the legacy acm-statistics database.
  Body (application/x-www-form-urlencoded): username=<username>
  Returns: application/pdf on success (Content-Disposition: attachment)
  Returns: text/html with error message if username not found or legacy data unavailable

POST {{ base }}/pdf/merge
  Merge two OJHunt Lite PDFs. Crawler settings and username come from PDF A.
  Body (multipart/form-data): pdf_a=<file>, pdf_b=<file>
  Returns: application/pdf on success (Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="merged.pdf")
  Returns: text/html with error message on invalid input

